At every step, only the current head of each list needs to be checked. Any later node in the same list is at least as large as its head. The code scans all heads, attaches the smallest one, and advances that list.

This avoids a heap and uses constant extra working space, but the scan can become slow when there are many lists. The input list of heads is updated as nodes are removed. Empty lists and an empty collection both work.

```java
import java.util.List;

/*Definition for singly Linked List
class ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode next;

    ListNode() {
        val = 0;
        next = null;
    }

    ListNode(int data1) {
        val = data1;
        next = null;
    }

    ListNode(int data1, ListNode next1) {
        val = data1;
        next = next1;
    }
}
*/

class Solution {
    public ListNode mergeKSortedLists(List<ListNode> heads) {
        if (heads == null || heads.isEmpty()) {
            return null;
        }

        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);
        ListNode tail = dummy;

        while (true) {
            int smallestIndex = -1;

            for (int index = 0; index < heads.size(); index++) {
                ListNode current = heads.get(index);

                if (current == null) {
                    continue;
                }

                if (smallestIndex == -1
                        || current.val < heads.get(smallestIndex).val) {
                    smallestIndex = index;
                }
            }

            if (smallestIndex == -1) {
                break;
            }

            ListNode smallest = heads.get(smallestIndex);
            tail.next = smallest;
            tail = smallest;
            heads.set(smallestIndex, smallest.next);
        }

        tail.next = null;
        return dummy.next;
    }
}
```

For lists `2 -> 6` and `1 -> 4`, the first scan picks `1`, then the heads become `2` and `4`. The next scan picks `2`, and so on.

Time complexity: `O(NK)` in the worst case, since each selected node may require checking all `K` heads.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the result links.

The heap keeps the first remaining node from each non-empty list. The smallest one is always the next node for the result. After taking a node, its next node becomes the new candidate from that list.

For example, with heads `1, 1, 2`, the heap removes `1`, then adds the next node from that same list. The process continues until no candidates remain. The existing nodes are connected directly, so the input lists are changed.

```cpp
#include <queue>
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    struct CompareNodes {
        bool operator()(ListNode* first, ListNode* second) const {
            return first->val > second->val;
        }
    };

    ListNode* mergeKSortedLists(vector<ListNode*> &head) {
        priority_queue<ListNode*, vector<ListNode*>, CompareNodes> minHeap;

        for (ListNode* node : head) {
            if (node != nullptr) {
                minHeap.push(node);
            }
        }

        ListNode dummy(0);
        ListNode* tail = &dummy;

        while (!minHeap.empty()) {
            ListNode* smallest = minHeap.top();
            minHeap.pop();

            tail->next = smallest;
            tail = smallest;

            if (smallest->next != nullptr) {
                minHeap.push(smallest->next);
            }
        }

        tail->next = nullptr;
        return dummy.next;
    }
};
```

With `[[1,4], [1,3], [2]]`, the heap starts with `1, 1, 2`. It outputs `1`, inserts its next node, and keeps choosing the smallest current head until the result is `1 -> 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4`.

Time complexity: `O(N log K)`, where `N` is the total number of nodes and `K` is the number of lists.
Space complexity: `O(K)` for the heap.

The lists are merged in balanced rounds. First, neighboring lists are joined. Then the new groups are joined again. This keeps a node involved in about `log K` rounds instead of merging every new list into one growing list.

The merge helper changes the existing links, so no new nodes are made for the result. An empty collection returns `null`, and a single list is returned as it is.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

/*Definition for singly Linked List
class ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode next;

    ListNode() {
        val = 0;
        next = null;
    }

    ListNode(int data1) {
        val = data1;
        next = null;
    }

    ListNode(int data1, ListNode next1) {
        val = data1;
        next = next1;
    }
}
*/

class Solution {
    public ListNode mergeKSortedLists(List<ListNode> heads) {
        if (heads == null || heads.isEmpty()) {
            return null;
        }

        List<ListNode> currentRound = new ArrayList<>(heads);

        while (currentRound.size() > 1) {
            List<ListNode> nextRound = new ArrayList<>();

            for (int index = 0; index < currentRound.size(); index += 2) {
                ListNode first = currentRound.get(index);
                ListNode second = index + 1 < currentRound.size()
                        ? currentRound.get(index + 1)
                        : null;
                nextRound.add(mergeTwo(first, second));
            }

            currentRound = nextRound;
        }

        return currentRound.get(0);
    }

    private ListNode mergeTwo(ListNode first, ListNode second) {
        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);
        ListNode tail = dummy;

        while (first != null && second != null) {
            if (first.val <= second.val) {
                tail.next = first;
                first = first.next;
            } else {
                tail.next = second;
                second = second.next;
            }
            tail = tail.next;
        }

        tail.next = first != null ? first : second;
        return dummy.next;
    }
}
```

A useful edge case is an odd number of lists. If one list has no partner in a round, it is copied into the next round unchanged. Lists that are empty are also safe because `mergeTwo` can accept `null` on either side.

Time complexity: `O(N log K)`.
Space complexity: `O(K)` for the round lists, with `O(log K)` levels of grouping.
